kind: perf
upstream: not for V-HACD (which its author considers deprecated): the hook is
trimkit's, so the library's fan-outs run on the extension's one pool.

`Compute` started a pool of 8 std::threads per call (`m_asyncACD`, which in
the synchronous implementation only means "use threads") and ran its three
fan-outs on it: splitting every pending hull, costing every pair of hulls
for the merge matrix, and costing a merged hull against the rest. Each is a
parallel-for: post every task, then wait for all of them. This replaces the
pool with one hook in `Parameters`, `m_parallelFor(context, count, body,
bodyContext)`, which src/vhacd.cpp points at the extension's scheduler
(src/parallel.hpp through common.hpp's `run_ranges`), and runs each fan-out
through `RunParallel`, in order on the calling thread when no hook is given.
The build defines VHACD_DISABLE_THREADING, so the ThreadPool, its futures
and the asynchronous implementation are not compiled at all.

Why: one pool for the whole extension, whose workers exit when idle, and
nothing hidden starting threads per call; an exception (std::bad_alloc, the
allocation door's MemoryError) thrown on a worker comes back through the hook
to the calling thread and out of `Compute` as a Python exception, where a
`std::packaged_task` on the library's own pool would have rethrown it at
`future::get` with the pool's threads still running.

The answers are the same: every task computes its own cost or split, and the
results are added to the priority queue in task order after the fan-out,
exactly as before, so nothing depends on which thread ran what.

Measured 2026-11-09 in the trimkit-dev image on 10 cores of a busy machine,
five alternating rounds, the best of each (`uv run scripts` dev build), V-HACD's
own pool against this hook:
  sphere, 14k faces, 400k voxels     2.923 s  ->  2.903 s
  sphere, 14k faces, 100k voxels     1.241 s  ->  1.305 s
  two disjoint boxes, 400k voxels    0.214 s  ->  0.165 s
  two disjoint boxes, 100k, 8 hulls  0.072 s  ->  0.063 s
Within the noise either way. The hook sets `Job::eager`: the scheduler
publishes and wakes before the first item, as the pool did, because these
fan-outs are a few heavy items (a hull split each) and measuring the first
alone ran each round as two batches, 25% slower overall.

Covered by: tests/test_vhacd.py (every output pinned or checked against the
library's own pool's), tests/test_threads.py (8 threads of decompose at once),
fuzz/vhacd.cpp.

